{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-user feed limit (`max_feeds`, 0 = unlimited). The build and the existing unit and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself has not been exercised by any test I could run.\n\n- **Schema and model:** new migration adds `users.max_feeds int not null default 0`; `User.MaxFeeds` and `UserModificationRequest.MaxFeeds` (`max_feeds` in JSON) are read and written by every user query in `internal/storage/user.go`.\n- **Enforcement:** `storage.CreateFeed` is the only place a feed row is inserted, so the check lives there. It locks the user row, counts the user's feeds and inserts in one transaction, returning `storage.ErrFeedLimitReached` at the limit. This covers the API, OPML import, the web UI and the Google Reader API, including concurrent requests.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`:** returns 400 when the limit is reached, checked before the feed is fetched, and again if the storage layer refuses.\n- **`POST /v1/import`:** imports feeds until the limit is reached, then stops and returns 400. Feeds imported before that point are kept, so the import is partial rather than all-or-nothing.\n- **`PUT /v1/users/{userID}`:** a non-admin sending `max_feeds` gets 403; a negative value gets 400.\n\nThings to know:\n- The web UI and Google Reader paths are blocked at the limit but surface it as a generic database error rather than a dedicated message.\n- `opml.Import` now reads the `users` table through storage, which is outside that package's `.agentpolicy` nouns (`categories, feeds`). I updated the effect tags on `Import` and `importFeedsHandler`; `opml.Export` already reads `users` the same way.\n- I did not add `max_feeds` to the Go `client` package's user types.", "t": 1794461923.004}
